# 📌 Important Notes

- A generator function contains at least one `yield` statement.
- Calling a generator function returns a **generator object**.
- Generator objects are iterators.
- Values are generated only when requested.
- Once a generator is exhausted, it cannot be restarted; a new generator object must be created.
- Generators are ideal for processing large datasets and streams of data.

---

# ⚖️ Generator vs Normal Function

| Normal Function | Generator Function |
|-----------------|--------------------|
| Uses `return` | Uses `yield` |
| Returns all at once | Returns one value at a time |
| Ends after `return` | Pauses after each `yield` |
| Higher memory usage | Memory efficient |
| Suitable for small data | Suitable for large or infinite data |

---

# ⚖️ List vs Generator

| List | Generator |
|------|-----------|
| Stores all values in memory | Generates values on demand |
| Faster for repeated access | Better memory efficiency |
| Uses square brackets `[]` | Uses parentheses `()` |
| Suitable for small datasets | Suitable for large datasets |

